{
  "summary": "P1 credential phishing. User Aisha Khan (HR Generalist, user_id 5, aisha.khan@brightline.example, device BL-LT-0105) reports clicking the link in a \"payroll verification\" email and entering her password on the site. She didn't give an exact click time. No attachment was mentioned. Related message: a quarantined copy Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I in place of l), subject \"Urgent: verify your payroll login\", received 2026-10-10T21:05Z, marked phishing. The copy she clicked presumably reached her inbox, so a copy may have got past the filter. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 sign-ins, all successful, all from New York (corporate 203.0.113.10 / residential 198.51.100.25), all with Duo push, all within usual hours. No hosting networks, no failed or denied MFA. The latest is 2026-10-09 13:35Z, which is before the phishing email arrived. I see no sign of attacker access so far, so I have NOT requested block_sign_in. Duo is enrolled (push, her phone). Actions: reset_password queued (approval #1). Temp password goes by phone after callback verification. Still needed: approve the reset; confirm click time with the user; security review of the sender/domain and a block for all users; check whether other mailboxes received it; keep watching sign-ins and block if anything suspicious shows up.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate; P1 credential compromise"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us so quickly. That really helps. We're treating the password you typed as stolen. I checked your account and don't see any sign that someone else has signed in. We've asked for your password to be reset. Once a technician approves it, they'll call you to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password over the phone (never by email). You'll change it the first time you sign in. Our security team is also looking into this right away. In the meantime: 1) Don't delete the email. Security may want to look at it. Don't forward it to anyone or reply to it. 2) If you use that same password anywhere else, change it there now. 3) Never approve a Duo push you didn't start yourself, and tell us right away if you get one. If you also opened an attachment from that email, disconnect your laptop from Wi-Fi and let us know.",
  "internal_note": "P1 phishing, credentials entered (KB-029). Checks: user active, Duo push enrolled. 14-day sign-in logs: 10 successful sign-ins, all from NY corporate/residential IPs, Duo push, within usual hours. No hosting networks, no failed or denied MFA. The latest sign-in (10-09 13:35Z) is before the phishing email arrived. No suspicious activity, so I did not request block_sign_in. Quarantine has a matching phishing message Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain), 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', 10-10 21:05Z, not releasable. The copy she clicked probably reached her inbox. Queued: reset_password (approval #1). Verify by callback and give the temp password by phone. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) for security review, a domain block, a check of other recipients, and ongoing sign-in monitoring. Click time not yet confirmed with the user.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
